<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Repeatable GUI checks · Umicom</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css">@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Running repeatable GUI checks</h1><p>When you test an application, it helps to follow the same steps each time. In Umicom Notes, you might save a document, read the saved file and check that a second document still has its unsaved changes. A <strong>scenario</strong> describes that sequence. A <strong>session</strong> is one attempt to run it.</p>
<p>The session keeps the whole plan, including steps that have not run. This matters when a check fails or someone cancels: the report should show where work stopped, rather than making unfinished checks disappear.</p>
<h2 id="notes">Begin with one Notes document</h2>
<p>The supplied example uses a temporary Notes file. It does not open or modify your project files. Its driver handles three targets: <code>notes.save</code>, <code>notes.saved-file</code> and <code>notes.other-draft</code>. A driver is the small adapter that knows how to perform an action or read a result.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Request Save</td><td>Call the Notes save handler.</td><td>A handler can return successfully without writing anything; this is only the request.</td></tr><tr><td>Verify the file</td><td>Read the temporary file and compare its bytes with the expected note.</td><td>This checks the saved result independently.</td></tr><tr><td>Verify the other draft</td><td>Read the separate unsaved draft.</td><td>Saving one document must not change another document by mistake.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="build">Build the example on Windows</h2><p>Open PowerShell in your Applications checkout. The small test build compiles the real Framework session sources without building every application. Run each command separately and read its result before continuing.</p><pre><code>Set-Location &quot;C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications&quot;
$env:Path = &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path&quot;
&amp; &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\cmake.exe&quot; -S framework/tests/gui_check_runs -B build/gui-check-runs -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=&quot;C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/gcc.exe&quot; -DCMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM=&quot;C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/ninja.exe&quot;
&amp; &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\cmake.exe&quot; --build build/gui-check-runs --parallel 2
&amp; &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\ctest.exe&quot; --test-dir build/gui-check-runs --output-on-failure --no-tests=error</code></pre>
<p>Run the normal example. Its JSON output should show <code>"accepted":true</code>, three attempted steps and no failed or unrun steps.</p><pre><code>&amp; &quot;.\build\gui-check-runs\umicom-check-session-example.exe&quot;
$LASTEXITCODE</code></pre>
<h3>Try a Save handler that does nothing</h3><p>The <code>--fail-save</code> option deliberately skips the write. The save request returns, but the next check reads an empty file. The process returns <code>1</code>, the session stops, and the third step remains <code>not-run</code>.</p><pre><code>&amp; &quot;.\build\gui-check-runs\umicom-check-session-example.exe&quot; --fail-save
$LASTEXITCODE</code></pre>
<h3>Cancel between steps</h3><p>The <code>--cancel</code> option requests cancellation after Save returns. No later check runs. The file operation has already happened; cancellation does not undo it. The exit code is <code>1</code> because this sequence has not been accepted.</p><pre><code>&amp; &quot;.\build\gui-check-runs\umicom-check-session-example.exe&quot; --cancel
$LASTEXITCODE</code></pre>
<h2 id="reports">Keep a report</h2><p>Create an empty directory for this run. The example refuses to overwrite existing report files. A successful export writes <code>checks.html</code>, <code>checks.json</code> and <code>junit.xml</code>.</p><pre><code>$Report = Join-Path $env:TEMP (&quot;umicom-notes-check-&quot; + (Get-Date -Format &quot;yyyyMMdd-HHmmss-fff&quot;))
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $Report
&amp; &quot;.\build\gui-check-runs\umicom-check-session-example.exe&quot; --output $Report
Start-Process (Join-Path $Report &quot;checks.html&quot;)</code></pre>
<p>HTML is the easiest format to read. JSON is useful for another programme that needs the results. JUnit XML can be read by build and test-reporting tools. The JUnit report includes one extra <strong>run-completion</strong> check. It fails when a run was cancelled, stopped early or is still running, so a report containing only skipped actions cannot appear successful.</p>
<p>The example returns <code>0</code> for accepted checks, <code>1</code> for a failed or cancelled run, and <code>2</code> for a setup or export problem. A disk-write failure is not evidence that the deliberately broken Save handler was detected.</p>
<h2 id="linux">The same exercise on Linux</h2><p>Start in the root of your Applications checkout.</p><pre><code>cmake -S framework/tests/gui_check_runs -B build/gui-check-runs -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build/gui-check-runs --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir build/gui-check-runs --output-on-failure --no-tests=error
./build/gui-check-runs/umicom-check-session-example
./build/gui-check-runs/umicom-check-session-example --fail-save
./build/gui-check-runs/umicom-check-session-example --cancel</code></pre>
<h2 id="states">Read the state before the totals</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ready</td><td>The plan has been copied, but no action has run.</td></tr><tr><td>Running</td><td>The run has started. More actions remain, or a driver callback is executing.</td></tr><tr><td>Completed</td><td>All planned actions were attempted. Inspect the failed count before deciding whether the application passed.</td></tr><tr><td>Stopped</td><td>A failed step stopped the sequence. Later steps remain in the report as not run.</td></tr><tr><td>Cancelled</td><td>A cancellation request prevented further actions. Completed actions and their results remain recorded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="privacy">Share only what is needed</h2><p>Reports omit titles, step values, driver messages and observed text by default. A developer can include them explicitly when needed. Identifiers remain in the report, so do not use account numbers, passwords or private document contents as control or scenario IDs.</p>
<p>Screenshots and interaction recordings remain separate evidence. A recording describes what a person tried. A repeatable check also needs known starting data and a clear expected result. Review those details before turning a recording into an automated scenario.</p>
<h2 id="application">Use the session in a graphical application</h2><p>Framework already defines <code>UmiUiAutomationDriver</code> for toolkit and test adapters. The new session accepts that same driver. It does not add a second command system or a second set of assertion rules.</p>
<p>Call <code>UmiUiAutomationSessionAdvance</code> from the application's owning thread to perform one step. Return to the event loop before requesting the next step. This leaves an opportunity to update progress and handle Cancel between actions. A driver callback can still block: cancellation cannot interrupt that callback, and a test launcher should enforce an overall process timeout.</p>
<p>The session API does not add a new Run button to the existing Report problem panel. A host connects its own selected scenario, progress display and cancellation action to these shared functions. There is no automatic conversion of recorded clicks into a replay script.</p>
<h2 id="contracts">Understand the contract</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function or type</th><th>Purpose and ownership</th><th>Where to see it used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionCreate</code></td><td>Copy a nonempty scenario and driver interface. The driver context is borrowed and must remain alive while steps can run.</td><td>Example: create the Notes session, then destroy the original scenario.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionAdvance</code></td><td>Attempt one step. An OK return means its result was recorded, not that the check passed.</td><td>Example: the owner-thread loop requests each Notes action.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionCancel</code></td><td>Prevent further actions; an active callback finishes first.</td><td>Example: the cancellation exercise stops after Save.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionDestroy</code></td><td>Release the session and report. It returns Busy during its driver callback.</td><td>Example: the cleanup block releases the session.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionReport</code></td><td>Borrow the report until session destruction. Do not destroy this borrowed pointer separately.</td><td>Example: use the same report for progress, exports and acceptance.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationReportSnapshot</code></td><td>Copy metadata and progress counters without executing an action.</td><td>Example: decide whether another step may run.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationReportStep</code></td><td>Copy a planned row and its state. A not-run row has no driver outcome.</td><td>Tests: inspect the last unrun row after stopping at the first failure.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationReportWriteJson / WriteHtml / WriteJUnit</code></td><td>Copy the report before writing it through the supplied output callback. The caller owns and closes the file.</td><td>Example: export the same result to three files.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationReportRequireSuccess</code></td><td>Require a nonempty completed plan, matching expected count and no failures.</td><td>Example: choose process exit code 0 or 1.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiUiAutomationSessionSnapshot</code></td><td>A copied view of the run. Planned equals attempted plus notRun; attempted equals passed plus failed.</td><td>Example: inspect the state. Tests verify these relationships throughout cancellation/failure combinations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="source-map">Find the implementation</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File or target</th><th>Responsibility and example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/automation_session.h</code></td><td>The new public session and report contracts. Start here before reading the implementation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/automation.h</code></td><td>The existing scenario, driver and assertion contracts. The Notes example supplies a small driver.</td></tr><tr><td><code>src/ui/automation.c</code></td><td>Owns the scenario, session and report state. Synchronous runs and paced sessions share one checked step executor.</td></tr><tr><td><code>src/ui/automation_report.c</code></td><td>Copies and exports the report. The failing-output tests show how an I/O error stops further writes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/gui_check_runs/test_sessions.c</code></td><td>Exercises normal, failed, cancelled, reentrant, copied-plan, malformed-text and export cases.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/gui_check_runs/test_allocation.c</code></td><td>Injects Linux allocation failures before actions or output. Earlier state remains intact.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/gui_check_runs/CMakeLists.txt</code></td><td>Builds the focused production-source tests or registers them in the complete Framework build.</td></tr><tr><td><code>examples/gui_recording/check_sessions.c</code></td><td>The complete Notes exercise shown below.</td></tr><tr><td><code>examples/gui_recording/check_session_result.cmake</code></td><td>Checks the example's exact exit code, state, counters and acceptance flag.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Umicom::ui</code></td><td>The Framework library to link from another application. GTK adapters remain behind their existing public boundary.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="source">Complete Notes example</h2><p>Read the <code>main</code> function first to see the sequence, then follow <code>PerformNotesStep</code> to see how the saved result is checked. The output callback writes bytes; it does not decide whether the application passed.</p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_CHECK_SESSIONS_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<h2 id="exercise">A small extension</h2><p>Add a fourth assertion that reads the saved file a second time. Run the normal, ineffective-Save and cancellation exercises again. Decide which rows should be attempted before looking at the report. Keep the expected result independent of the operation that writes the file.</p></main></body></html>